WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.910
Film Courage: What’s the formula for writing
a great story?

00:00:01.910 --> 00:00:08.210
Dr. Ken Atchity, Author/Producer: I think
that a great story starts with finding a character

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:16.750
in a strange situation and working that character
out of that situation.

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:23.350
Faulkner said that that’s the way his novel
started is that a character haunted him and

00:00:23.350 --> 00:00:30.920
he allowed it to haunt him until questions
started coming and he gave the example of

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:39.670
a novel I wrote once started with a girl sitting
in a tree with muddy pants and her knees were

00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:49.340
bruised and she’s looking in the window
of the house from the tree and she’s crying.

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:55.140
And so I started asking questions about why
were her knees bruised, why were her knees

00:00:55.140 --> 00:01:01.050
not bruised and it’s because she climbed
this tree since she was little and didn’t

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:03.789
have any problem climbing the tree.

00:01:03.789 --> 00:01:05.390
Why were her pants dirty?

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:11.189
Well because her brother pushed her in the
creek and that’s why she ran home and why

00:01:11.189 --> 00:01:12.619
was she looking in the window?

00:01:12.619 --> 00:01:15.289
Because she was told not to come in the house.

00:01:15.289 --> 00:01:16.709
What was she looking at in the window?

00:01:16.709 --> 00:01:22.899
Well it was the funeral of her grandfather
and so on.

00:01:22.899 --> 00:01:28.549
And when he had all of those questions he
could then answer those questions.

00:01:28.549 --> 00:01:33.711
The think about Faulkner that kind of illustrates
my view of creativity is he didn’t write

00:01:33.711 --> 00:01:34.829
this down.

00:01:34.829 --> 00:01:41.039
You know I always say if you trust your mind,
the story will form itself in your mind.

00:01:41.039 --> 00:01:45.780
The minute you start writing it down, you
are interfering with your mind because you

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:51.409
are now dealing with pieces of paper and we
start feeling possessive about pieces of paper

00:01:51.409 --> 00:01:52.929
and we want to do something with them.

00:01:52.929 --> 00:01:56.989
We want to put them in order but what if one
of these pieces of paper isn’t a good one

00:01:56.989 --> 00:01:57.989
at all?

00:01:57.989 --> 00:01:59.249
Well he will forget that.

00:01:59.249 --> 00:02:02.209
He will forget the thing about her knees.

00:02:02.209 --> 00:02:04.509
For example if that’s not a good one.

00:02:04.509 --> 00:02:10.000
But he leaves it all in his mind because that
way it’s free to form (almost like an embryo)

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.200
any way it wants to form.

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:16.900
And once it’s fully formed then you sit
down and write it out.

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:23.319
I always said people will never have writer’s
block at all if they simply never sit down

00:02:23.319 --> 00:02:28.709
to write until they know what they are going
to write when they sit down.

00:02:28.709 --> 00:02:33.310
And if you know that in advance then you just
start dashing it out.

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:38.310
That’s why you limit your time 45 minutes
to an hour and at the end of it you can’t

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:39.310
wait to write more.

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:43.920
Perfect go to tomorrow and do it then because
that energy will be there already whereas

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:49.689
if you sort of run out of things to write
you’ve misused your time management.

00:02:49.689 --> 00:02:55.769
So I think that’s how a story starts is
by some character that haunts you until you

00:02:55.769 --> 00:03:01.780
have to write about it and then you go an
apply all the other rules about storytelling

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:06.900
on top of that but basically it starts with
the character and no matter how great a writer

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:12.390
you are if you don’t have an intriguing
character at the heart of it doesn’t matter

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:14.999
because you’re not going to hold your reader.

00:03:14.999 --> 00:03:17.129
You’re not going to hold your audience.

00:03:17.129 --> 00:03:19.200
The audience just wants to see people.

